  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 1146 AM MST Wed Nov 11 2026\n\nThis weekend will mark a transition period as a slightly more \nprogressive pattern takes place after another week of anomalously \nwarm and dry conditions. Saturday will start off much cooler than \nthe previous day, as a cold front is expected to push across SE \nWyoming and the Nebraska Panhandle during the morning hours. Not \nonly will this result in forecast highs anywhere from 10-15 degrees \ncooler from the previous day, but northerly winds will increase as \nan inverted ridge sets up east of the Continental Divide. The \nincreasing gradient flow as a result of a potent 1030 mb + \nanticyclone located over the Canadian Prairies will drive strong \ncold advection behind the front throughout the day, although no \nwidespread precipitation is expected due to the main large-scale \nforcing being well to our south. While we certainly can't rule out \nsome light snowfall especially within the Snowy and Sierra Madre \nMountains, 50th percentile ECMWF ensemble QPF for SE Wyoming is \ncurrently sitting below 0.05\" and has trended downward over the last \ncouple of runs. This seems reasonable given that the primary \nshortwave trough and associated DCVA will be focused more over \nSouthern Colorado. \n\nGFS, Canadian, and ECMWF ensemble guidance are all in fairly good \nagreement that our region will be located in a region of upper-level \ndiffluence on Sunday, downstream of an amplified trough located off \nthe West Coast. This will place SE Wyoming and W Nebraska in a SW \nflow regime, with current LREF guidance suggesting PWAT values will \nsituate as high as the 85th-95th percentile (0.4-0.5\") relative to \nclimatology. While this sounds great for us in desperate need of \nbeneficial precipitation, mid and upper-level height rises \nassociated with a building shortwave ridge, plus the lack of any \nforcing mechanism unfortunately point to mostly dry conditions. This \nis similar to much of what we have seen this fall given an active \nmonsoonal pattern, yet we haven't been able to translate much of \nthis moisture down to the surface as precipitation. Temperatures \nlook to be at or slightly above average for this time of year, \nmainly in the low to mid 50s east of the Laramie Range, with \nslightly cooler to the west. \n\nWhat once looked slightly more active and exciting has now started \nto look, well...dry and boring, as we move into the beginning of \nnext week. The downstream ridge remains quasi-stationary over the \nGreat Plains, whereas the upstream troughing begins to dig south and \neast towards Southern California. This will continue to SW surge of \nmonsoonal moisture, which will unfortunately be confined primarily \nto the mid and upper levels of the atmosphere. As a result, SE \nWyoming and W Nebraska will remain mostly dry and seasonably mild, \nwith high temperatures on Tuesday and Wednesday 5 to 10 degrees \nabove average for this time of year.",
